  "status": "published",
  "title": "After California’s Bullet Train Budget Debacle, a Texas Democrat Wants Texas to Build Its Own",
  "deck": "Rep. John Bucy’s proposal would open the door to state funding for a privately operated Dallas-to-San Antonio rail line. The bill leaves the state’s share of the cost open.",
  "category": "texas",
  "categoryLabel": "Texas",
  "author": "EP News",
  "type": "Article",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-23",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-23",
  "body": [
    "Texas law generally bars the state from paying a private company to build or run high-speed rail. State Rep. John Bucy, an Austin-area Democrat, wants to repeal that restriction and require the Texas Department of Transportation to hire a private company to build a line from Dallas to San Antonio, with stops in Waco and Austin.",
    "California voters heard a similar pitch in 2008, when they approved nearly $10 billion in bonds for a rail system then expected to cost about $33 billion. The California High-Speed Rail Authority now estimates it would cost $231.3 billion in today’s dollars to build the full San Francisco–Los Angeles–Anaheim system. The authority puts the cost of a scaled-back version reaching the Los Angeles Basin at $126.1 billion.",
    "Bucy’s House Bill 270 calls for trains capable of reaching at least 110 mph along the Interstate 35 corridor. The private company that builds the line would also maintain it and run the trains. The bill makes no appropriation and sets no cap on what the state could contribute. It leaves open who would cover construction overruns or operating losses.",
    "What would it cost to get from Austin to San Antonio?",
    "Two studies have looked at that stretch of the route. The biggest difference between their estimates comes down to whose tracks the trains would use.",
    "TxDOT studied running more passenger trains on Union Pacific’s existing freight line. It estimated that $810 million to $1.9 billion in improvements, including sidings where one train could pull over to let another pass, could support up to eight passenger round trips a day. Union Pacific has questioned how it could fit that many passenger trains around its freight traffic.",
    "Building a separate passenger route between the two cities could cost $13.5 billion, according to a study commissioned by Travis County. That estimate covers only Austin to San Antonio. Bucy’s proposal would still have to get passengers from Austin through Waco to Dallas.",
    "Austin City Council Member Vanessa Fuentes and San Antonio City Council Member Jalen McKee-Rodriguez have been pushing for passenger service between their cities. For someone who regularly drives that stretch of I-35, eight round trips a day would offer a real choice. Whether the cheaper option can deliver that schedule depends in part on an agreement with the railroad that owns the tracks.",
    "California voters knew the size of the bond they were approving, even as the projected cost of the railroad outgrew it. Texas lawmakers could put a dollar limit in Bucy’s bill before they vote. As written, it would repeal the existing funding restriction without specifying how much of the bill Texas taxpayers could ultimately pick up."
  ],
